we're going to begin this hour with the  senate republican minority whip john  thune who joins us at the table to talk  about the battle over abortion rights  after that stunning and stunning as a  word the leak from the supreme court on  roe v wade he says a democratic push to  enshrine a right to an abortion into  federal law goes too far and is out of  step with most americans but just last  week a cbs news poll found that 64  percent of americans do not want roe v  wade overturned senator john thune we're  happy to say joins us at the table we  love it when you come into the studio  it's nice to have you in person i want  to talk to you about your vote last week  you voted against uh to codify roe v  wade why did you vote that way what was  your thinking well i've been i have a  history in my time in public life of of  being pro-life um but that vote uh was  more than just codifying roe uh which is  why  uh they lost even people who typically  support roe versus wade on our side  people like senators colin murkowski  collins and murkowski but basically it  was more expansive it went beyond  roe and it was a piece of legislation i  think they knew was going to fail we had  that vote actually a few weeks ago  and i think senator schumer did it to  satisfy  a political constituency that wanted  that vote but knew it was going to fail  he thinks it's going to galvanize  democrats in the midterms right is there  any concern on the republican side that  this issue could in fact galvanize  democrats well i think that they're  looking for something to distract from  the economy inflation and the border and  some of the issues that they would  rather not talk about  um i think this is an issue this is a  pivot clear pivot on their part to try  and get on something that they think  they can play offense with but a lot of  it's going to come down to in in the  politics around this issue is is very  contentious always has been fraught is a  good word  on both sides it is and and so how that  plays politically uh remains to be seen  because there are many places around the  country obviously where  you were if you're a candidate running  and you're a pro-life candidate that's  going to work to your political  advantage but most americans it seems uh  support uh roe v wade would you support  it with some restrictions  well i think to your point gail if you  look at the way this polls um if you you  know roe versus wade the basically the  decision the supreme court decision said  the state's interest becomes compelling  at the point of viability which keeps  moving back and you know right now um 65  percent of americans don't think you  ought to have abortions past the first  trimester so into the second or third  trimester 80 percent believe you  shouldn't have them after the second  trimester and and we're really out of  step with the world i mean we're one of  only seven countries in the world that  allows abortions after 20 weeks and  those include of course north korea and  china most european nations france is at  14 weeks  germany's at 12 weeks so  most countries have found a political  consensus around restrictions  that are i guess would start much sooner  in a pregnancy is that where you are  yeah so why not support  something that had those restrictions in  those timelines that you speak of i  think the question for a lot of people  is going to be is it a human life and  when is that when does life begin um i  think what you're going to see is state  legislatures because this doesn't this  doesn't do away with it it just returns  it to the state state level are going to  have to find political consensus and  some states are probably going to define  it differently my position has always  been exceptions rape incest life of the  mother but a ban i'm wondering if you're  still enjoying your job and i'm asking  you that because there's a bestselling  book out right now i know you've heard  of it this will not pass it says you  considered not running for re-election  this year but senate minority leader  mitch mcconnell said hold on just a  second senator so first are you enjoying  your job and why did you is it true that  you considered not running for  re-election  is that a good smile i've got well i've  got you know like you i've got grandkids  now around  um  it was it was a hard hard decision but  in the end you you do things because you  think it's some place you can make a  difference and i'd like to believe  public life is still a place where you  can make a difference  in in going back to the beginning  discussion there's one thought we  there's we hear so much about as you  talk about when does life begin there's  a lot of of talk of  what the rights are of be it a fetus  be it an unborn child however you refer  to that what about the women and the  families that we're talking about and  their rights too how do you balance  that in the consideration well i think  you have to you got to be compassionate  and you know the the solution if you're  a pro-life person uh and i am  and if you believe that's a human life  then you believe it needs to be  protected but there's also there is  there's a mom and a lot of vulnerable  moms and i think that  um the responsibility for people who  hold a pro-life position isn't to just  to state that check the box you've got  to actually demonstrate that you're  willing to help care for moms who are  going to carry carry babies to uh  uh to the point of birth or give moms a  chance to have that say potential moms  to control some of what is their body  also trust in them and in doctors also  one of the questions is well go ahead  well no i mean i think that you you're  right and and that's why  you know as you look at this issue as i  said you've got to do it in a way i  think there's a compassionate way to  understand  that your impact now i'm not i'm i'm a  middle middle-aged white guy i'm  probably not the person should be  talking about a subject like this but i  also have a wife and two very  accomplished daughters who are pro-life  and but you're a middle-aged white guy  in a position of power to make a  difference on something  and i think you have to listen you have  to listen very carefully what people are  saying but in the end if i'm going to  air i'm going to err on the side of life  um but i think in how you  determine the course of action  is going to be largely based upon where  the political consensus is and i think  you know right now this is a decision  that's been made by nine unelected  judges and it's now going to go back to  the people and their elected  representatives which is i think where  a lot of folks think issues like this  should be decided it's a very big deal  for a government to tell a woman she has  to carry a pregnancy through the term  yeah i think we all agree on that and  would you tell some of your other  middle-aged white men that we like  middle-aged white men here at the table  spread the word we're very friendly here  i feel like all like all points i mean  like everybody that comes very like all  points of view that comes when you're  close to calling me middle aged and i i  still i still claim to be youthful  thank you very much

